<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The app's themes.

  They used to live in the manifest, pointing straight at a Material style.
  With two form factors —phone and watch— that no longer works: the watch needs
  attributes that can only be set in a style of its own, so both themes are
  declared here and each manifest picks its own.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
      The phone's. It is still Material 3 with no action bar, which is exactly
      what the manifest said before: the whole screen is drawn by the app and
      the layout already reserves the gap for the status bar.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.AngularNative" parent="Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar" />

    <!--
      The watch's.

      Wear OS has no light mode worth having: the screen is OLED and what is
      not painted draws no power, so a black background is not an aesthetic
      preference but battery life. That is why this one is fixed `Dark` and not
      `DayNight`.

      `windowSwipeToDismiss` is the watch's "back": there is no button, it is
      dragged from the left edge. The system's Wear themes
      —`Theme.DeviceDefault`— come with it set; Material 3's, which are meant
      for the phone, do not. Without this line the app has no way out, and the
      user has nothing to get wrong: simply nothing happens.

      `Theme.DeviceDefault` is not used directly, which is what Google
      recommends for a Wear app built with views, because `MainActivity` is an
      `AppCompatActivity` and AppCompat refuses to start on a theme that does
      not descend from `Theme.AppCompat`. It is the same Activity for both form
      factors, and splitting it in two over the theme would cost more than it
      fixes.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.AngularNative.Wear" parent="Theme.Material3.Dark.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowSwipeToDismiss">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/black</item>
    </style>

</resources>
